A '''Poké Maniac''' (Japanese: '''かいじゅうマニア''' ''{{wp|Kaiju|Monster}} Maniac''; also known as '''マニア''' ''Maniac'' in [[Generation I]]), formatted '''Pokémaniac''' in Generations I to III, is a type of [[Pokémon Trainer]] that first debuted in the [[Generation I]] games. They were originally presented as strange boys with long hair wearing trenchcoats. In [[Generation III]], they were changed to {{pkmn|cosplay}}ers dressing up in Pokémon-like costumes. In [[Generation VII]], Poké Maniacs cosplay as {{p|Slowpoke}} and, when defeated, imitate Slowpoke with their faces. They are named for their enthusiasm for "{{wp|Kaiju|Kaiju}}"-esque Pokémon,<ref>Kaiju Mania Club, [https://www.pokemon.jp/special/kaijumania/zadankai/ かいじゅう座談会], [[Pokémon Daisuki Club]]</ref> and tend to use Pokémon in the {{egg3|Monster}}, such as {{p|Lairon}}, {{p|Lickitung}}, and {{p|Charmander}}.

In [[Pokémon: Let's Go, Pikachu! and Let's Go, Eevee!|Let's Go, Pikachu! and Let's Go, Eevee!]], Poké Maniacs will award three {{i|Poké Ball}}s along with [[prize money]] upon being defeated.

==Terminology==
In Generations I to III, this Trainer class is referred to as '''POKéMANIAC'''. In guidebooks, which often present these names in sentence case, it is often formatted as '''Pokémaniac'''<ref>[[Official Pokémon Special Pikachu Edition Perfect Guide]] p. 64</ref><ref>[[Pokémon Gold & Silver: Prima's Official Strategy Guide]] p. 43</ref>.

From Generation IV onward, this Trainer class is named '''Poké Maniac'''.

==Notable Poké Maniacs==
In {{5v2|Gold|Silver|Crystal|HeartGold|SoulSilver}}, [[Cianwood City#Shuckie's owner's house|Kirk]] (known as Mania in [[Generation II]]), a Poké Maniac from [[Cianwood City]], is well known for his arrogance. Hearing of this, {{ga|Silver}} stole one of his two Pokémon, a {{p|Sneasel}}. Kirk begs the player to take Shuckie, his {{p|Shuckle}}, for safekeeping. Depending on how well Shuckie is treated, Kirk may or may not ask for Shuckie to be given back.

Aside from the Trainer class, the term '''Poké Maniac''' (Japanese: '''ポケモンマニア''' ''Pokémon Maniac'') has also been used to refer to people that are deeply interested in rare Pokémon, most notably [[Bill]] and his father. However, in original Japanese version, this term is different from the class and does not carry the implication of liking Kaiju-like Pokémon. Probably for this reason, Bill's title was re-translated to '''Pokémon fanatic''' in the English version of [[Pokémon: Let's Go, Pikachu! and Let's Go, Eevee!]].

